// Doc-tests — the Bit-native port of tests/docs.zig (#351).
//
// The property, in one sentence: every ```` ```bit ```` fenced block under
// `docs/` that is not marked `ignore` typechecks against the real prelude and
// the real `std/*` — with a page's blocks joined into ONE module unless the page
// carries `<!-- doctest: per-block -->`.
//
// Documentation that does not compile is worse than no documentation, because a
// reader trusts it. A snippet is CHECKED, not built: it is a module, not a
// program, so it has no `main` to link. That is exactly what lets a reference
// page show one function in isolation.
//
// A page's blocks are one module by default, concatenated in document order,
// because that is how a reference page reads: `interfaces.md` declares
// `interface Shape` in one block and implements it three blocks later. It also
// means such a page cannot declare a name twice. A page whose blocks are
// separate programs — a tutorial, where each snippet is a whole `main` you could
// run — says so with `<!-- doctest: per-block -->`, and each of its blocks is
// checked on its own. A block that cannot join its page's module is fenced
// ```` ```bit ignore ````: still highlighted as Bit, just not checked.
//
// WHAT THE PORT CHANGES, AND THE NEW FAILURE MODE IT CREATES. The Zig original
// called the front end in-process (`bit.checkHostProject`) and got the
// diagnostics back as a value. Bit gets one subprocess primitive — ABI.md §19's
// `os_run_bounded(path, timeout_ms)`, no argv and no stdout capture — so argv,
// the `BIT_STDLIB` override and the output redirect all have to come from a
// generated `/bin/sh` script. That is a way to be green while checking nothing
// which did not exist before the port: a script that cannot fail makes every
// page pass. `plumbingSelfTest` runs FIRST and exists solely to kill that: it
// checks one snippet that must pass and one that must fail, and aborts the whole
// run if the broken one comes back clean.
//
// WHY THE CHECKS RUN IN PARALLEL, AND HOW THE VERDICT SURVIVES IT. Serially
// this gate cost 303s, which is why it was wired out of `./make test`. The
// cost is neither the harness nor process startup: it is the front end
// re-checking `std/*` once per snippet, so one page importing `std/http` costs
// 2.8s against 0.03s for one importing nothing. No process can share that work,
// so the only lever is running them at once — and `osRunBounded` cannot, being
// `@nosplit` over module-level scratch, one child at a time. So the fan-out
// lives in the script, and ONE `osRunBounded` call drives the whole corpus.
//
// The unit of judgement does NOT change: every snippet still gets its own module
// directory, `bit check`, captured output and exit status, and a failure is
// still named by page and by block. What changes is where the status comes from.
// `exec` no longer makes the script's status BE the compiler's — there are N
// compilers — so each job `echo`es its own into `s<i>`, and a MISSING or
// unparseable `s<i>` is a hard abort, never a pass. `plumbingSelfTest` runs its
// good and bad snippets as one two-job batch through that exact path, which also
// pins the slot mapping: put both in slot 0 and the bad one comes back clean,
// put both in slot 1 and the good one fails.
//
// The batch deadline replaces the per-check one and stays a real bound: ~48s
// against `BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S`'s 300s default is the ~6x headroom docs/development.md sets
// for every other harness. A timeout is still attributable — jobs that finished
// wrote a status, so the ones missing one are the snippets that hung.
//
// #2422: on a host running several concurrent `./make test`s, the whole batch
// can miss that 300s deadline even though no single snippet is broken — the
// deadline is measuring contention on the box, not the corpus. The old
// rendering turned that into one `docs: FAIL ... does not typecheck` per
// unjudged block, so a busy Mac reported 100-200 "broken" doc pages that were
// never checked at all. A batch timeout is now ONE harness-level
// `docs: INCONCLUSIVE` outcome naming how many blocks were judged out of how
// many were queued — never per-block spam — and it still exits non-zero: an
// unjudged corpus is not a pass, whether the cause is load or a genuine hang.
// A block that DOES get judged (its status file exists) keeps failing by name
// exactly as before; only the ones the deadline cut off change how they are
// reported.
//
// WHY THE SCRIPT IS WRITTEN ONCE AND NEVER REWRITTEN. Rewriting an executable
// while a previous exec of it may still be in flight is the ETXTBSY trap that
// bit tests/harness.zig (74811a3). Only data changes: the slot modules
// `w<i>/main.bit` and the job count in `n.txt`, which the script reads.
//
// WHY THE COMPILER IS COPIED BEFORE BEING RUN. The self-hosted `bit` is
// rewritten in place by a `has_side_effects` build step, so a concurrent build
// truncates the file mid-exec (#1644); a truncated binary execs with no output,
// which a "did it report errors" assertion would happily read as success. The
// per-run nonce in the scratch path is #1828's fix for the fixed-/tmp-path race.
//
// FLOORS, NOT A `> 0` FLOOR. `checked > 0` — the Zig original's only corpus
// assertion — cannot tell 271 blocks from 1. A cursor that fails to advance past
// a close fence yields exactly one block per page and still passes it. The
// floors below are set just under the live corpus (271 blocks over 31 pages,
// 219 invocations) so a structural regression in the extractor lands under them.

//
// #2969: split across a sibling to stay under the 800-line lint limit. This
// file owns the corpus — what a page is, what a block is, the extractor and
// its own unit tests, the corpus walk, the floors, and `main`'s reporting
// (including the #2422 `docs: INCONCLUSIVE` denominator line). The child-
// process runner — the scratch tree, the copied compiler, the generated
// fan-out script, and reading back each slot's verdict — moved to
// `docsrunner/runner.bit`, a separate module this file imports from.

import { walk } from "std/fs"
import { contains, indexOf, hasSuffix } from "std/strings"
import {
  Cfg,
  CheckResult,
  die,
  slurp,
  jsonish,
  repoRoot,
  trimInfo,
  timedOutCode,
  maxJobs,
  runBatch,
  setup,
  teardown,
  plumbingSelfTest,
} from "./docsrunner"

// Bounds on the walk, so the traversal is provably finite (Power of 10).
const maxFiles: int = 256

const maxBlocksPerFile: int = 128

const fenceTag: string = "```bit"

const fenceClose: string = "\n```"

// A page carrying this marker has its blocks checked one at a time, rather than
// joined into a single module.
const perBlockMarker: string = "<!-- doctest: per-block -->"

// Corpus floors. Each is set below today's real count with room for ordinary
// documentation churn, and far above the value any known extractor regression
// would produce.
const minPagesWithBlocks: int = 25
const minTotalBlocks: int = 250
const minInvocations: int = 200

// One snippet queued for checking. `block` is the 1-based block number for a
// per-block page, or 0 when the job is the page's blocks joined — the two cases
// read differently in a failure message.
struct Job {
  page: string,
  block: int,
  blocks: int,
  src: string,
}

// The corpus walk's output: what to check, and what was counted while deciding.
struct Plan { jobs: []Job, st: Stats }

// One extractBlocks unit case.
struct ExtractCase {
  name: string,
  md: string,
  want: []string,
}

// The checkable `bit` blocks in `md`: those fenced exactly ```` ```bit ```` at
// the start of a line. A port of the Zig extractor, decision for decision.
//
// The two tests that look redundant and are not: `atLineStart` rejects a
// ```` ```bit ```` written inside prose (docs/stdlib/README.md has one), and
// `len(info) == 0` rejects both ```` ```bit ignore ```` and a hypothetical
// ```` ```bitrot ````, which shares the tag as a prefix.
fn extractBlocks(md: string): []string {
  let blocks = []string(0)
  let rest = md
  while (len(blocks) < maxBlocksPerFile) {
    let tagRel = indexOf(rest, fenceTag)
    if (tagRel < 0) {
      break
    }
    let t = int(tagRel)
    let atLineStart = t == 0 || rest[t - 1] == '\n'
    let afterTag = rest[t + len(fenceTag):]

    // The rest of the fence line is the info string's remaining words.
    let nlRel = indexOf(afterTag, "\n")
    if (nlRel < 0) {
      break
    }
    let n = int(nlRel)
    let info = trimInfo(afterTag[:n])
    let body = afterTag[n + 1:]

    let closeRel = indexOf(body, fenceClose)
    if (closeRel < 0) {
      break
    }
    let c = int(closeRel)
    if (atLineStart && len(info) == 0) {
      blocks = append(blocks, body[:c])
    }
    // Advancing to the close fence — not past the open one — is what stops a
    // nested ```` ```bit ```` inside a block being re-read as a new block, and
    // is the line whose absence yields exactly one block per page.
    rest = body[c:]
  }
  return blocks
}

// A page's blocks, joined into one module in document order.
fn joinBlocks(blocks: []string): string {
  let out = ""
  for b of blocks {
    out = out + b + "\n\n"
  }
  return out
}

// The extractor's own tests, on literal pages rather than the corpus. The
// corpus floors prove the extractor finds a lot; these prove it finds the RIGHT
// things, which no count can.
fn extractCases(): []ExtractCase {
  return [
    // The Zig original's case: takes ```bit, skips ```bit ignore and a bare
    // fence, and the two results prove the cursor advances past a close fence.
    ExtractCase{
      name: "takes-bit-skips-ignore-and-plain",
      md: "intro\n```bit\nlet a = 1\n```\nprose\n```\nnot bit\n```\n```bit ignore\nthis need not compile\n```\n```bit\nlet b = 2\n```\n",
      want: ["let a = 1", "let b = 2"],
    },
    // docs/stdlib/README.md's live shape: a ```bit written inside prose. Its
    // info string is empty in neither reading, but the at-line-start test is
    // what must reject it — and the following real block must still be found.
    ExtractCase{
      name: "inline-fence-is-not-a-block",
      md: "x ```bit\nnope\n```\n```bit\nlet ok = 1\n```\n",
      want: ["let ok = 1"],
    },
    // CRLF. The info string is "\r", which only trimInfo (not std/strings'
    // trimSpace) reduces to empty. Get this wrong and a CRLF page silently
    // contributes zero blocks.
    ExtractCase{
      name: "crlf-page",
      md: "```bit\r\nlet a = 1\r\n```\r\n",
      want: ["let a = 1\r"],
    },
    // ```bitrot shares the tag as a prefix and is not a Bit fence at all.
    ExtractCase{
      name: "bitrot-is-not-bit",
      md: "```bitrot\nlet a = 1\n```\n",
      want: [],
    },
    // An unterminated fence is dropped, not half-read.
    ExtractCase{
      name: "unterminated-fence",
      md: "```bit\nlet a = 1\n",
      want: [],
    },
  ]
}

fn runExtractTests(): int {
  let cases = extractCases()
  // A shortened case list is a green run that proves nothing.
  assert(len(cases) == 5)

  let failed = 0
  for c of cases {
    let got = extractBlocks(c.md)
    if (len(got) != len(c.want)) {
      eprint("docs: FAIL extractBlocks/${c.name}: got ${len(got)} block(s), want ${len(c.want)}\n")
      failed = failed + 1
      continue
    }
    let i = 0
    let ok = true
    while (i < len(got)) {
      if (got[i] != c.want[i]) {
        eprint("docs: FAIL extractBlocks/${c.name}: block #${i + 1} was ${jsonish(got[i])}, want ${jsonish(c.want[i])}\n")
        ok = false
      }
      i = i + 1
    }
    if (!ok) {
      failed = failed + 1
    }
  }
  return failed
}

// Every .md file under `docsDir`, in walk order and bounded by maxFiles.
fn docPages(docsDir: string): []string {
  let all = walk(docsDir) catch e {
    // Never swallowed into an empty list: an empty corpus passes every
    // assertion that is not a floor.
    die("cannot walk ${docsDir}: ${e.message()}")
    []string(0)
  }
  if (len(all) > maxFiles) {
    die("${docsDir} holds ${len(all)} entries, over the maxFiles bound of ${maxFiles}")
  }
  let pages = []string(0)
  for p of all {
    if (hasSuffix(p, ".md")) {
      pages = append(pages, p)
    }
  }
  return pages
}

// What the corpus walk observed. Every field is asserted afterwards; none is
// merely reported.
//
// `judged` + `unjudged` always sum to `invocations`: every queued job lands in
// exactly one of "got a real compiler verdict" or "the batch deadline cut it
// off before it wrote one". `failures` counts only among the judged — an
// unjudged block is never scored as a content failure (#2422).
struct Stats {
  pages: int,
  pagesWithBlocks: int,
  pagesWithoutBlocks: int,
  totalBlocks: int,
  invocations: int,
  failures: int,
  judged: int,
  unjudged: int,
}

// One page's jobs: one per block for a per-block page, otherwise one for the
// whole page with its blocks joined in document order.
fn pageJobs(page: string, md: string, blocks: []string): []Job {
  let out = []Job(0)
  if (contains(md, perBlockMarker)) {
    let i = 0
    while (i < len(blocks)) {
      out = append(out, Job{ page: page, block: i + 1, blocks: len(blocks), src: blocks[i] })
      i = i + 1
    }
    return out
  }
  return append(out, Job{ page: page, block: 0, blocks: len(blocks), src: joinBlocks(blocks) })
}

// Walks the corpus and queues every snippet, checking nothing. Extraction is
// separated from execution so the runner gets one full queue — a per-page
// fan-out would idle every worker on the 30 pages that yield a single job.
fn planCorpus(pages: []string): Plan {
  let st = Stats{
    pages: len(pages), pagesWithBlocks: 0, pagesWithoutBlocks: 0,
    totalBlocks: 0, invocations: 0, failures: 0, judged: 0, unjudged: 0,
  }
  let jobs = []Job(0)
  for page of pages {
    let md = slurp(page, "doc page")
    let blocks = extractBlocks(md)
    if (len(blocks) == 0) {
      st.pagesWithoutBlocks = st.pagesWithoutBlocks + 1
      continue
    }
    if (len(blocks) >= maxBlocksPerFile) {
      die("${page} hit the maxBlocksPerFile bound of ${maxBlocksPerFile}; blocks past it are silently unchecked")
    }
    st.pagesWithBlocks = st.pagesWithBlocks + 1
    st.totalBlocks = st.totalBlocks + len(blocks)
    for j of pageJobs(page, md, blocks) {
      if (len(jobs) >= maxJobs) {
        die("more than ${maxJobs} snippets queued; the corpus outgrew this harness's bound")
      }
      jobs = append(jobs, j)
    }
  }
  return Plan{ jobs: jobs, st: st }
}

// A failure, named by page and by block — the whole point of batching
// carefully: the message must be what it was one-snippet-per-process.
fn reportFailure(j: Job, r: CheckResult) {
  if (j.block > 0) {
    eprint("\ndocs: FAIL ${j.page} block #${j.block} does not typecheck (status ${r.code}):\n${r.report}\n--- snippet ---\n${j.src}\n")
    return
  }
  eprint("\ndocs: FAIL ${j.page} (${j.blocks} blocks, joined) does not typecheck (status ${r.code}):\n${r.report}\n--- joined module ---\n${j.src}\n")
}

// Every queued job, in one batch. Results come back in submission order, so
// result i is job i — the mapping the plumbing self-test pins before this runs.
fn runJobs(cfg: Cfg, jobs: []Job, st: Stats): Stats {
  let out = st
  let srcs = []string(0)
  for j of jobs {
    srcs = append(srcs, j.src)
  }
  let rs = runBatch(cfg, srcs)
  // A short result list would silently drop the tail of the corpus.
  assert(len(rs) == len(jobs))

  // A block the batch deadline cut off before it wrote a status is unjudged,
  // not failed: it is tallied and named once, in the harness-level
  // `docs: INCONCLUSIVE` summary (#2422), never through `reportFailure` — that
  // is what stops one elapsed deadline from becoming N per-block FAIL lines.
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(jobs)) {
    out.invocations = out.invocations + 1
    if (rs[i].code == timedOutCode) {
      out.unjudged = out.unjudged + 1
    } else {
      out.judged = out.judged + 1
      if (rs[i].code != 0) {
        out.failures = out.failures + 1
        reportFailure(jobs[i], rs[i])
      }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out
}

// The floors, each naming what it would have caught — a bare number in a
// failure message is a puzzle rather than a diagnosis.
fn checkFloors(st: Stats): int {
  let floored = 0
  if (st.pagesWithBlocks < minPagesWithBlocks) {
    eprint("docs: FLOOR: only ${st.pagesWithBlocks} page(s) yielded blocks, want >= ${minPagesWithBlocks} — the corpus shrank or the fence match broke\n")
    floored = floored + 1
  }
  if (st.totalBlocks < minTotalBlocks) {
    eprint("docs: FLOOR: only ${st.totalBlocks} block(s) extracted, want >= ${minTotalBlocks} — a cursor that stops after the first block per page lands here\n")
    floored = floored + 1
  }
  if (st.invocations < minInvocations) {
    eprint("docs: FLOOR: only ${st.invocations} check invocation(s), want >= ${minInvocations} — per-block pages are being joined, or pages are being skipped\n")
    floored = floored + 1
  }
  if (st.pagesWithoutBlocks * 2 >= st.pages) {
    eprint("docs: FLOOR: ${st.pagesWithoutBlocks} of ${st.pages} page(s) yielded nothing — the info-string test is rejecting real fences\n")
    floored = floored + 1
  }
  return floored
}

fn main() {
  let cfg = setup(repoRoot())

  // Unit first: a broken extractor makes every corpus number meaningless.
  let unitFailures = runExtractTests()
  if (unitFailures != 0) {
    teardown(cfg)
    eprint("docs: FAILED — ${unitFailures} extractBlocks unit case(s)\n")
    osExit(1)
  }

  // Plumbing second, and before a single page is read.
  plumbingSelfTest(cfg)

  let pages = docPages(cfg.docsDir)
  if (len(pages) == 0) {
    teardown(cfg)
    eprint("docs: FAILED — no .md files under ${cfg.docsDir}\n")
    osExit(1)
  }

  let plan = planCorpus(pages)
  let st = runJobs(cfg, plan.jobs, plan.st)
  teardown(cfg)

  // #2422: an elapsed batch deadline is ONE harness-level outcome, reported
  // and exited on before floors are even evaluated — floor violations here
  // would just be noise restating "the batch didn't finish", and this is the
  // one place the whole fix exists to guarantee: never one FAIL per unjudged
  // block, always exactly this one line, always with its denominator.
  if (st.unjudged != 0) {
    eprint("docs: INCONCLUSIVE — batch deadline of ${cfg.timeoutMs}ms elapsed with ${st.judged}/${st.invocations} block(s) judged (${st.unjudged} unjudged) over ${st.pages} page(s) in ${cfg.docsDir} — host load or a genuine hang, re-run to tell them apart\n")
    osExit(1)
  }

  let floored = checkFloors(st)
  if (st.failures != 0 || floored != 0) {
    eprint("docs: FAILED — ${st.failures} page/block failure(s), ${floored} floor violation(s) over ${st.pages} page(s) in ${cfg.docsDir}\n")
    osExit(1)
  }
  print("docs: ok — ${st.totalBlocks} block(s) over ${st.pagesWithBlocks} page(s), ${st.judged}/${st.invocations} check(s) run, ${st.pages} .md file(s) in ${cfg.docsDir}\n")
}
